WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.655
Picture a café owner who wants one new number on her screen: how many different cards paid at her café today.

2
00:00:09.655 --> 00:00:12.527
A regular who buys three coffees counts once.

3
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:21.067
The company keeps numbers like this in Redis, a program that holds data in memory and answers commands from other programs.

4
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.658
The obvious answer is a list of every card that paid.

5
00:00:24.658 --> 00:00:26.961
It's exact, and tiny for one café.

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:30.742
But there are 20,000 shops, counted every hour.

7
00:00:30.742 --> 00:00:36.270
And the list grows with every new card: 100,000 cards took about 4.4 MB.

8
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.650
Can you count without the list?

9
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.109
Hold that question.

10
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:44.598
Try the simplest counter: INCR, add one, on every payment.

11
00:00:44.598 --> 00:00:46.786
It counts payments, not cards.

12
00:00:46.786 --> 00:00:49.048
The regular counts three times.

13
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:53.045
To skip the repeats, you must remember which cards you've seen.

14
00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:54.376
That's the list again.

15
00:00:54.376 --> 00:00:58.853
An exact count of different things needs memory that grows with the count.

16
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:01.237
So let's give up a little exactness.

17
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:07.001
Picture a fair, where you want to know how many people came, without writing down a single name.

18
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:13.423
You ask each visitor to flip a coin until it lands heads, and to tell you how many tails came first.

19
00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:17.508
You keep one number: the longest run of tails anyone has reported.

20
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.668
A run of 10 tails turns up about once in 1,024 tries.

21
00:01:21.668 --> 00:01:26.759
So if your record is 10, about a thousand people have probably come.

22
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:30.311
It's the laziest head count ever taken, and it works.

23
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.617
In code, the coin flips come from a hash function.

24
00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:39.531
It turns any text into a number that looks random, and the same text always gives the same number.

25
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:41.755
Read its bits from the right.

26
00:01:41.755 --> 00:01:43.707
A 0 is a tail, a 1 is heads.

27
00:01:43.707 --> 00:01:47.053
Count the zeros before the first 1, and add one.

28
00:01:47.053 --> 00:01:48.656
That's the hash's rank.

29
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:53.603
A rank of r turns up about once in 2 to the power r different cards.

30
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.787
Now the regular pays again.

31
00:01:55.787 --> 00:01:58.093
Does her card push the record up?

32
00:01:58.093 --> 00:01:59.211
Pause and guess.

33
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:02.669
No.

34
00:02:02.669 --> 00:02:05.427
A visitor who flips again gets a new run.

35
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:09.397
A card that pays again has the same hash, so the same rank.

36
00:02:09.397 --> 00:02:13.501
Here the fair picture breaks, in our favor: repeats are free.

37
00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:16.125
One record is a wild guess, though.

38
00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:21.247
A single lucky card with 20 zeros would make you believe in a million cards.

39
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:22.733
So keep many records.

40
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:28.584
Split the cards into groups by a few bits of their hash, and keep the highest rank in each group.

41
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:29.336
Card:A.

42
00:02:29.336 --> 00:02:33.079
Its lowest three bits, 0 1 1, pick register 3.

43
00:02:33.079 --> 00:02:36.659
The rest ends in one zero, then a 1: rank 2.

44
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:39.394
Card:B lands in register 5, rank 1.

45
00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:41.881
Half of all hashes look like that.

46
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.577
Card:C also lands in register 3, with three zeros: rank 4.

47
00:02:46.577 --> 00:02:49.376
The register keeps the larger, so 4.

48
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.002
And card:A pays again: same register, rank 2, and 2 isn't above 4.

49
00:02:55.002 --> 00:02:56.312
Nothing changes.

50
00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.895
After thousands of cards, each register holds the longest run it saw.

51
00:03:00.895 --> 00:03:07.073
A kind of average combines them, one that stops a lucky register from pulling the answer too far.

52
00:03:07.333 --> 00:03:10.773
Redis does this with 16,384 registers.

53
00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:14.755
Fourteen bits of the hash pick the register.

54
00:03:14.755 --> 00:03:20.367
A rank fits in 6 bits, so all the registers take 12,288 bytes.

55
00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:23.104
That structure is a HyperLogLog.

56
00:03:23.104 --> 00:03:36.900
It takes at most 12 KB, and its standard error is 0.81 percent: about two times in three, the estimate is within 0.81 percent of the truth, for a thousand cards or a billion.

57
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.527
The commands start with PF, for Philippe Flajolet, who invented the method.

58
00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:46.073
PFADD adds cards, and PFCOUNT gives the estimate.

59
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:53.440
On the lab server, 100,000 cards went into a set and into a HyperLogLog.

60
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.303
The set says exactly 100,000.

61
00:03:56.303 --> 00:04:01.436
The HyperLogLog says 101,058: 1.06 percent too high.

62
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:03.596
And the memory?

63
00:04:03.596 --> 00:04:07.523
The set takes 4,361,843 bytes.

64
00:04:07.523 --> 00:04:14.197
The HyperLogLog takes 14,364, about 300 times less.

65
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:20.678
In exchange, it can't list the cards it has seen, and it can't say whether one card is among them.

66
00:04:20.678 --> 00:04:21.634
It only counts.

67
00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:24.565
So use it when one percent either way is fine.

68
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:25.816
One more gift.

69
00:04:25.816 --> 00:04:37.132
PFMERGE keeps the larger value of each register, so 24 hourly counters merge into one for the day, and a card that paid in two hours still counts once.

70
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.057
So the moral of the story: you don't need a list to count.

71
00:04:41.057 --> 00:04:46.416
Keep the longest runs, and the count comes out of them, in 12 KB, whatever the crowd.

72
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:47.942
One quick check.

73
00:04:47.942 --> 00:04:50.813
The owner asks: did card:A pay today?

74
00:04:50.813 --> 00:04:52.909
Can the HyperLogLog answer?

75
00:04:52.909 --> 00:04:54.151
Pause and think.

76
00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:57.612
No.

77
00:04:57.612 --> 00:05:00.939
It kept only the longest runs, never the cards.

78
00:05:00.939 --> 00:05:03.841
It can count them, but it can't name one.
